    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Eureka Roadhouse began in 1936 when Ma and Pa Warrick built a 15‑by‑15‑foot log cabin on a lake shore near Eureka Summit.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/eureka_roadhouse_alaska"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Eureka Roadhouse has served as a lasting waypoint on Alaska’s early 20th‑century trail system, echoing the frontier era of mining and homesteading.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/eureka_roadhouse_alaska"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "From its summit, Eureka Roadhouse offers panoramic views of the Chugach Mountains and the Matanuska Valley, drawing hikers and anglers alike.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/eureka_roadhouse_alaska"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "During World War II, Eureka Roadhouse became a critical stopover as Alaska’s military infrastructure expanded rapidly.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/eureka_roadhouse_alaska"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Eureka Roadhouse sits in a subarctic climate zone, experiencing long, cold winters and short, mild summers.",
